--- RipsEdgeCountProfile.lean
+++ RipsEdgeCountProfile.lean
@@ -74,4 +74,33 @@
         Set.ncard_le_ncard (Set.subset_univ _) (Set.toFinite _)
     _ = Fintype.card (Sym2 α) := by rw [Set.ncard_univ]; exact Nat.card_eq_fintype_card
 
+#check edgeCountProfile_mono
+#check edgeCountProfile_zero
+
+/-
+-- !-- Lab Notes -- !--
+
+Hypothesis. The edge count of a finite Rips graph should be a monotone numerical
+summary of its filtration, beginning at zero and remaining uniformly bounded by
+the finite space of unordered vertex pairs.
+
+Experiment. The monotonicity theorem follows by transporting graph inclusion to
+edge-set inclusion and then comparing finite cardinalities. The zero-scale and
+finite-pair bounds test the two extremal regimes directly.
+
+Analysis. The resulting profile is a coarse but reusable bridge from metric geometry
+to finite combinatorics. A broader generalization would index the profile by real
+scales and record its jump values rather than sampling only integer thresholds.
+
+Critique. Edge counts do not determine homology or even graph isomorphism; distinct
+Rips graphs may have identical profiles. The boundary at scale zero also relies on
+a genuine metric space: in a pseudometric space, distinct points can have distance
+zero.
+
+Synthesis. The profile supplies a monotone, bounded observable that complements the
+complex-level perturbation results, while its limitations prevent it from being
+mistaken for a sphere-recognition invariant.
+-- !--
+-/
+
 
